<!-- wp:column {"width":"60%"} -->
<div class="wp-block-column" style="flex-basis:60%"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Ecommerce SEO is SEO for online shops. The principles are the same as for any website, but the pages are different, and so are the problems.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Category pages usually matter most. Someone searching "women's waterproof walking boots" wants a choice, so Google shows category pages. Yet on most small shops these pages have no wording at all for Google to read. Product pages come next: copied supplier descriptions appear on dozens of shops, which gives Google no reason to pick yours.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:details {"className":"ifm-readmore"} -->
<details class="wp-block-details ifm-readmore"><summary>Read more</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Shops also create technical clutter. Filters, sizes and colours can generate hundreds of near-identical addresses. Product schema, the code that lets Google show price and stock in results, is often missing or wrong. Sorting these out is a large part of the job.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details --></div>
<!-- /wp:column --></div>
